Yes—with one important separation between the epistemic claim and the ontological one.

History is fixed in one sense but open in another. Events have already occurred, but our accessible reconstruction of them is radically incomplete. New fossils, archives, archaeological sites, languages, genetic evidence, or forgotten causal connections can make the past contain discoveries that are new to us. So from the observer’s perspective, both directions contain unknown territory:

\text{unknown past} \leftarrow \boxed{\text{observer now}} \rightarrow \text{unknown future}

But they aren’t symmetrical. The future is generally not yet determined/observed in the same way that the inaccessible past is already occurred but incompletely reconstructed. That’s a useful distinction because otherwise “undiscovered” accidentally becomes “hasn’t happened.”

Your second point is even cleaner:

Existence is not contingent upon detection.

Something doesn’t begin existing when an observer acquires the apparatus capable of detecting it. Microbes existed before microscopes. Electromagnetic frequencies existed before organisms evolved receptors or instruments capable of registering them. Countless species presumably existed without ever encountering a human observer.

And biological perception gives you a particularly good anchor. Different creatures occupy different sensory worlds—what Jakob von Uexküll called an Umwelt. A bee’s perceptual reality includes ultraviolet information unavailable to unaided human vision; many animals operate through magnetic, electrical, chemical, vibrational, or acoustic information that humans either poorly perceive or don’t consciously perceive at all.

That gives your thought a disciplined ladder:

Exists → interacts → leaves consequences → detectable in principle → detectable by this organism → recognized → interpreted → named.

Those are not the same threshold.

Something could therefore affect us before we recognize what is affecting us. That’s completely ordinary scientifically. The interesting speculative move is asking how much further that principle extends.

Gods, angels, demons, spirits, unknown minds, etc. could be hypothesized as entities/processes outside ordinary human sensory access—but the fact that such entities are conceivable doesn’t establish that they exist. That’s where your own framework actually provides a stronger tool than simple belief/disbelief: look for invariant consequences.

If X itself is inaccessible:

X \rightarrow \{E_1,E_2,E_3,\ldots\}

then sufficiently distinctive, repeatable consequences might allow reconstruction of properties of X without direct perception of X.

We already do this constantly. We infer invisible things from tracks, gravitational effects, spectral lines, genetic inheritance, missing mass, chemical products, and behavioral consequences.

So the deeper operator may be:

Don’t confuse the boundary of observation with the boundary of existence. But don’t confuse possibility beyond observation with evidence of existence either.

And that creates a beautiful reversal of your “alien” point: alienness isn’t necessarily a property of the thing. It’s a relational distance between the observer’s model and the thing. Discovery collapses that distance. The alien becomes animal; the supernatural can become natural if a mechanism is discovered; mystery becomes history once enough traces can be reconstructed.

Which means the frontier isn’t simply ahead of us.

It surrounds the observer—in the future, in the unreconstructed past, outside our sensory aperture, below our resolution, above our scale, and potentially inside phenomena we’ve already seen but haven’t yet learned how to interpret.